================================================================================
USED AT RUN TIME, NOT DISTRIBUTED
================================================================================

HexLinux relies on parts of the system it runs on. None of them is shipped
with HexLinux: each comes from the distribution, under its own licence (as
stated in the distribution's copyright file, Ubuntu 24.04 here).

libpulse (PulseAudio client library)
  Licence: LGPL-2.1-or-later
  Loaded at run time, from the system, through libpulse-simple.so.0 and
  libpulse.so.0: the microphone and the tones. PipeWire serves the same
  interface through pipewire-pulse.

Programs HexLinux runs as separate processes, when they are installed:
  wl-clipboard (wl-copy, wl-paste)  GPL-3.0-or-later   clipboard, Wayland
  xclip                             GPL-2.0-or-later   clipboard, X11
  xsel                              X11 licence        clipboard, X11
  xdotool                           BSD-3-Clause       keystrokes, X11
  wtype                             MIT (Expat)        keystrokes, wlroots Wayland
  loginctl (systemd)                LGPL-2.1-or-later  session state
  xdg-open (xdg-utils)              MIT (Expat)        opening the settings file
                                                       and the log folder
  notify-send (libnotify)           LGPL-2.1-or-later  notifications, when the
                                                       session bus cannot be
                                                       reached directly


================================================================================
INSPIRATION
================================================================================

HexLinux is the Linux sibling of HexWin (https://github.com/legb78/hex_windows),
by the same author, and follows its design, rules and diagnostics. Both take
their interaction model from Hex (https://github.com/kitlangton/Hex), a
dictation app for macOS, along with its choice of engine — Parakeet rather
than Whisper. No code is taken from Hex: it is written in Swift.
